# The Unicode Character Database, as questions a program asks of a character
# and of a string: which general category a code point is in, what its upper,
# lower, title and folded case are, whether a byte sequence is well-formed
# UTF-8, how a string reads in UTF-16, and the four normalization forms.
#
# The tables between `# tables-begin` and `# tables-end` are written by
# `scripts/generate_unicode_std.py` from the UCD data the repository already
# carries (`VERSION` says which release) and, for Cased and Case_Ignorable,
# from `DerivedCoreProperties.txt` of the same release; they are not to be
# edited by hand.
# Each is a string read in place by `RisCodeTable`, so a program starts with
# nothing to decode, and every lookup is a binary search over rows of two,
# three or four integers.
#
# What it answers, all under `Unicode.`:
#   * `letter?`, `number?`, `mark?`, `whitespace?`, `control?`, `lowercase?`,
#     `uppercase?`, `titlecase?`, `ascii?` — the general categories, exact:
#     `control?` is Cc, Cf, Cs and Co; `whitespace?` is Zs, Zl and Zp.
#   * `general_category` and `script` of a `Char` — `"Lu"`, `"Po"`, `"Cn"`
#     for unassigned; `"Greek"`, `"Common"`, `"Unknown"` — and
#     `property_ranges`, the code points of a category, a major class (`"P"`)
#     or a script as sorted `{from, to}` pairs, which `std/regex` builds
#     `\p{..}` from.
#   * `upcase`, `downcase`, `titlecase`, `foldcase` of a `Char` — the simple
#     one-to-one mappings; with a block, the full mappings (`ß` upcases to
#     `S`, `S`). `CaseOptions::ASCII` touches only ASCII, `Turkic` maps the
#     dotted and dotless i, `Fold` makes `downcase` fold.
#     `case_orbit` is every character with the same simple folding (`k`,
#     `K` and the Kelvin sign), what a case-insensitive match accepts, and
#     `each_case_orbit` yields every orbit of more than one.
#   * `upcase`, `downcase`, `capitalize`, `foldcase` of a `String` — the full
#     mappings, and `downcase` writes the final sigma: a `Σ` that ends a word
#     becomes `ς`.
#   * `valid?` and `clean` — whether bytes are well-formed UTF-8 (overlong
#     forms, surrogates, code points past U+10FFFF and truncated sequences are
#     not), and the same bytes with every maximal ill-formed subpart replaced
#     by U+FFFD.
#   * `encode_utf16` and `decode_utf16` — UTF-16 code units as `Array(UInt16)`.
#   * `normalize` and `normalized?` — NFD, NFC, NFKD and NFKC, with canonical
#     ordering by combining class and algorithmic Hangul.
#   * `graphemes`, `each_grapheme` and `grapheme_size` — the extended
#     grapheme clusters of UAX #29, every rule through GB9c's Indic
#     conjuncts, on `Unicode.` and on `String` itself: `"🇹🇷🇩🇪".graphemes` is
#     two flags. A piece of ill-formed UTF-8 is a cluster of its own.
#
# What it refuses: a string that is not well-formed UTF-8 is not case-mapped,
# encoded or normalized, it panics with the byte offset ("invalid UTF-8 at
# byte 3"); an unpaired surrogate in UTF-16 is `nil` from `decode_utf16?` and
# a panic from `decode_utf16`.
module std/unicode

import std/int

pub struct Unicode
